{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 17, :comments ["<b>And these are the years of Ishmael's life.</b> The straightforward approach is that Scripture recounts the generations and the years of the children of the righteous, to make known that the seed of the righteous is blessed. And it did not recount the days of Esau because he lived longer than Jacob, and the narrative was already completed with the death of Jacob, and it did not wish to go back to the years of Esau, since it had already mentioned his generations in the place fitting for them. And in the midrash of our Sages, regarding the recounting of the years of Ishmael, there are many reasons (see Megillah 17a), and the most correct among them is that he was a righteous man, a master of repentance, and Scripture recounted him in the manner of the righteous." "<b>And he expired (וַיִּגְוַע).</b> This is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Genesis 25:17): the term גְּוִיעָה is said only of the righteous. And in the Gemara (Bava Batra 16b) they raised an objection: but regarding the generation of the Flood, the term גְּוִיעָה is said of them — \"And all flesh expired (וַיִּגְוַע) that creeps upon the earth\" (Genesis 7:21), and \"all of mankind\"; \"all that is in the earth shall expire (יִגְוָע)\" (Genesis 6:17). And they answer: גְּוִיעָה and אֲסִיפָה (gathering) is what we are saying. Their intent is that גְּוִיעָה is death without painful illness and without suffering, and none merit it except the righteous. And the men of the generation of the Flood were overturned in an instant and hands did not afflict them with illness, and likewise the dead of the desert, and therefore the term גְּוִיעָה is said of them: \"when our brothers expired (בִּגְוַע אַחֵינוּ)\" (Numbers 20:3), and likewise \"and he was one man who did not expire (לֹא גָוַע) in his iniquity\" (Joshua 22:20) — meaning his iniquity did not kill him suddenly. But when Scripture mentions it together with the mention of death, like the expression וַיֵּאָסֵף (\"and was gathered\") or וַיָּמָת (\"and died\"), it alludes to the death of the righteous. And the language of (Bereshit Rabbah 62:1): \"And Abraham expired (וַיִּגְוַע) and died (וַיָּמָת)\" (Genesis 25:8) — Rabbi Yehudah bar Ilai said: the early pious ones would suffer intestinal illness for ten or twenty days, to demonstrate that illness scours. Rabbi Yehudah says: everyone of whom גְּוִיעָה is said died of intestinal illness. And there (Genesis 6:17) they said: \"all that is in the earth shall expire (יִגְוָע)\" — it shall shrivel. And it appears that the term גְּוִיעָה to them means \"his flesh shall rot (הָמֵק) while he is still standing on his feet\" (Zechariah 14:12), and so is the view of Onkelos (Onkelos on Genesis 25:17), who translates here \"וְאִתְנְגִיד\", which is wasting away, as in the expressions אִתְנְגִיד and אִתְּפַח (Sanhedrin 39a), \"perhaps he may pay five נְגִידִים\" (Bava Kamma 67a). And this term is likewise said of the Flood, as He said (Genesis 7:23) \"And He wiped out (וַיִּמַח) every existing thing,\" and it says \"and he expired and died (וַיִּגְוַע וַיָּמָת)\" — as a man weakens and dies, and this is death among the righteous."], :book :genesis, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 25, :notes {1 "In the phrase 'like a man weakens and dies,' the Hebrew כְּגֶבֶר יֶחֱלָשׁ וְיָמוּת is rendered literally; the subject and referent are somewhat compressed in the original."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:35:04.743584687Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}